1. Posts JSON structure
The endpoint returns a JSON array of post objects. Each object has userId, id, title, and body; the body can contain newline characters. JSONPlaceholder posts
2. GET users in Postman
I can’t capture a screenshot from your Postman app here. To get one: set the method to GET, enter https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/users, and click Send. The response is a JSON array of user objects, with fields such as id, name, username, email, address, phone, and company. Click Headers to view response headers and Body to view the JSON, then take your screenshot. JSONPlaceholder users
3. Fetch the first comment
async function fetchFirstComment() {
  const response = await fetch(
    "https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/comments"
  );

  if (!response.ok) {
    throw new Error(`Request failed: ${response.status}`);
  }

  const comments = await response.json();
  const firstComment = comments[0];

  console.log("Name:", firstComment.name);
  console.log("Email:", firstComment.email);
}

fetchFirstComment();
4. POST a new post in Postman
Set the method to POST and use https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/posts. Under Body, select raw and JSON, then enter:
{
  "title": "My first API post",
  "body": "This post was sent using Postman.",
  "userId": 1
}
JSONPlaceholder’s documented example returns 201 Created and includes the submitted fields plus a generated id (typically 101). The API simulates creating the post; it does not persist it. JSONPlaceholder guide
5. API rate limits
An API rate limit caps how many requests a client can make in a given time window. GitHub’s REST API sends 403 Forbidden or 429 Too Many Requests when a primary limit is exceeded; its response headers include the remaining request count and reset time. For example, once the limit is exhausted, further requests receive one of those errors until the reset time. GitHub REST API rate limits

 